{%- comment -%}
  Product videos, between the questions and the reviews.

  Sourced from Shopify product media, so clips are added and removed under the
  product's Media in the admin and this file never needs editing. Nothing is
  hardcoded and no external host is involved.

  No duplication with the gallery: that loop reads product.images, which holds
  images only, so a video lives here and only here. That is also why this is a
  section rather than a gallery slide. The gallery is what a shopper swipes to
  check the print; a clip in it means an accidental tap starts a video mid
  browse. A video is something you choose to watch.

  Absent, not empty, when the product has no video.

  No name, price, saving or Add to Cart. All four are already on this page
  above and below; repeating them turns a reel strip into a card grid arguing
  with the buy box about the same decision. The videos are the content.

  muted, loop and playsinline are the three attributes that let a clip start
  on its own. autoplay was briefly a fourth and is deliberately gone; the
  block above the <video> explains what it cost and what replaced it.

  Every clip the shopper can see is meant to be running, and still is. What
  changed is when the browser is told to fetch one: theme.js starts a clip as
  it approaches the viewport instead of the browser fetching all of them while
  the page is still parsing. On a phone the clips actually running are the
  first card and most of the second, which is what it was before — the
  difference is the other clips no longer cost anything until scrolled to.

  preload="none" rather than "metadata": with no src attached there is nothing
  to fetch a header for either, so this is belt and braces rather than the
  load-bearing part. width and height still come off the poster, so nothing
  shifts, and the poster is what fills the frame until playback starts — a
  real first frame rather than a black rectangle.

  No `controls` attribute, and that is the whole of the visual argument here.
  The native control bar is the one part of this page that no stylesheet owns:
  Chrome draws a dark rounded pill with a timeline, a volume slider, a
  fullscreen arrow and a three-dot download menu; Safari draws a translucent
  glass bar with an AirPlay target; iOS adds its own full-width play badge.
  Three browsers, three widgets, none of them ours, all of them roughly a
  third of the height of a 187px-wide reel. The parts of that bar that CSS can
  reach (`::-webkit-media-controls-*`) are non-standard, WebKit and Blink only,
  and silently do nothing in Firefox — so styling it cannot produce one
  presentation, only four slightly different ones.

  Nor does anything of ours replace it in the strip. A revision before this
  drew a pause disc and a sound disc over each frame, and even hidden until
  tapped they were the wrong idea: a 212px card is a thumbnail, and putting
  playback controls on a thumbnail asks the shopper to operate something they
  have not yet chosen to watch. Controls belong where the watching happens.

  So the strip is clips and nothing else, and a tap opens the viewer below —
  one clip, full screen, with the two controls that mode actually needs:
  sound, and a way out. That is also the honest place for sound, since a strip
  where any of four frames might start talking is worse than a viewer where
  one obviously will.

  What this deliberately gives up: scrubbing, playback rate, the browser's own
  fullscreen and AirPlay/Cast targets, and picture-in-picture. Long-press on
  iOS can still raise a system media menu — that one is not ours to remove.
{%- endcomment -%}
{{ 'section-product-videos.css' | asset_url | stylesheet_tag }}

{%- assign video_count = 0 -%}
{%- for m in product.media -%}
  {%- if m.media_type == 'video' -%}{%- assign video_count = video_count | plus: 1 -%}{%- endif -%}
{%- endfor -%}

{%- if video_count > 0 -%}
  <section class="sec sec--tight pvid-sec {% render 'surface-class', s: section.settings.surface %}"
           id="jump-{{ section.id }}"
           {% if section.settings.jump_label != blank %}data-jump="{{ section.settings.jump_label | escape }}"{% endif %}
           {% if section.settings.heading == blank %}aria-label="Product videos"{% endif %}>
    <div class="wrap">
      {%- if section.settings.heading != blank -%}
        <h2 class="h2">{{ section.settings.heading }}</h2>
      {%- endif -%}

      <ul class="pvid" data-pvid>
        {%- assign vidx = 0 -%}
        {%- for m in product.media -%}
          {%- if m.media_type == 'video' -%}
            {%- assign vidx = vidx | plus: 1 -%}
            {%- if m.alt != blank -%}
              {%- assign vlabel = m.alt -%}
            {%- else -%}
              {%- assign vlabel = 'Product video ' | append: vidx -%}
            {%- endif -%}

            {%- comment -%}
              Shopify transcodes every upload into several mp4 renditions
              (SD-480p, HD-720p, HD-1080p). A <video> with one <source> per
              rendition only ever plays the first one it meets that it can
              play — not the best one — so which rendition showed up here was
              an accident of API return order rather than a choice. Explicit
              is safer than implicit: smallest width for the strip, since a
              212px thumbnail gains nothing from 1080p; largest for the
              viewer, since that is a full-screen frame someone tapped to see
              closely.

              Neither URL is written into a <source> element any more — both
              are handed to JS as data attributes — so no mime type is
              carried alongside them. A src set from script needs no type
              hint: the type attribute exists to let a browser skip a
              candidate it cannot play without fetching it, and there is only
              ever one candidate here.
            {%- endcomment -%}
            {%- assign strip_src = '' -%}
            {%- assign strip_width = 999999 -%}
            {%- assign viewer_src = '' -%}
            {%- assign viewer_width = 0 -%}
            {%- for source in m.sources -%}
              {%- if source.format == 'mp4' -%}
                {%- if source.width < strip_width -%}
                  {%- assign strip_src = source.url -%}
                  {%- assign strip_width = source.width -%}
                {%- endif -%}
                {%- if source.width > viewer_width -%}
                  {%- assign viewer_src = source.url -%}
                  {%- assign viewer_width = source.width -%}
                {%- endif -%}
              {%- endif -%}
            {%- endfor -%}

            {%- comment -%}
              The source is a data-src, and the clip carries neither autoplay
              nor a real src until it is close to being seen.

              What that fixes: this section sits below the buy box, the
              benefits, the specs and the FAQ, so on every device none of
              these clips is ever on screen at first paint. With the autoplay
              attribute the browser began downloading all of them anyway —
              measured from Shopify's own rendition data, 4.7 MB on the
              alphabets mat and 7.8 MB on the cars mat — and theme.js then
              paused every one for being off screen. The bytes were spent in
              full, during the render of the page, and none of them were
              wanted yet. That is the remaining weight the audit's 17 MB
              figure was mostly made of.

              preload="none" alone would not have done it: for a video the
              browser considers autoplay-eligible, autoplay wins over
              preload, which is why the attribute has to come off rather
              than be counterweighted.

              The UX is unchanged and deliberately so — every clip still
              starts by itself, silent, looping, with no play button to
              press. It starts when it is nearly in view rather than when
              the HTML parses, which on this page is the first moment
              anybody could see it. theme.js already drove exactly this
              transition: it has always called play() on clips entering the
              viewport and paused them on the way out, so this reuses a
              path that was load-bearing before, not a new one. Muted plus
              playsinline is what makes a scripted play() permitted on iOS,
              and that combination is preserved.
            {%- endcomment -%}
            <li class="pvid-item">
              <div class="pvid-frame" data-pvid-frame data-pvid-label="{{ vlabel | escape }}">
                <video class="pvid-video"
                       muted
                       loop
                       playsinline
                       preload="none"
                       {%- if strip_src != blank %} data-pvid-strip-src="{{ strip_src }}"{% endif -%}
                       disablepictureinpicture
                       disableremoteplayback
                       x-webkit-airplay="deny"
                       {% if m.preview_image %}poster="{{ m.preview_image | image_url: width: 720 }}" width="{{ m.preview_image.width }}" height="{{ m.preview_image.height }}"{% endif %}
                       aria-label="{{ vlabel | escape }}"></video>

                {%- comment -%}
                  One transparent target over the whole frame, and nothing
                  drawn on top of the clip at all. A tap opens the viewer
                  rather than acting on playback: in the strip the clip is a
                  thumbnail that happens to be moving, and the controls belong
                  where someone has actually chosen to watch.

                  It is a real button rather than a click handler on the div,
                  so it is reachable by keyboard and announces what it does.
                {%- endcomment -%}
                <button type="button" class="pvid-open"
                        data-pvid-open
                        data-pvid-poster="{% if m.preview_image %}{{ m.preview_image | image_url: width: 1080 }}{% endif %}"
                        {%- if viewer_src != blank %} data-pvid-src="{{ viewer_src }}"{% endif -%}
                        aria-label="Watch {{ vlabel | escape }}"></button>
              </div>
            </li>
          {%- endif -%}
        {%- endfor -%}
      </ul>
    </div>

    {%- comment -%}
      The viewer. One of these for the section rather than one per clip: only
      one can ever be open, and a single <video> that has its source swapped
      means only one decoder and one buffer exist no matter how many clips the
      merchant uploads.

      hidden rather than display:none in a class, so the whole subtree is out
      of the accessibility tree and out of the tab order until it opens
      without depending on a stylesheet having loaded.

      Two controls, and the reasoning for each. Sound, because muted is what
      bought the autoplay in the strip and this is where someone has asked to
      watch. Close, because a full-screen layer with no visible way out is a
      trap on a phone, where there is no Escape key. Nothing else: a short
      looping clip has nowhere to seek to, and a viewer you opened on purpose
      does not need a play button.
    {%- endcomment -%}
    <div class="pvid-lb" data-pvid-lb hidden role="dialog" aria-modal="true" aria-labelledby="pvid-lb-title-{{ section.id }}">
      <h2 id="pvid-lb-title-{{ section.id }}" class="visually-hidden">Product video viewer</h2>

      <video class="pvid-lb-video"
             data-pvid-lb-video
             loop
             muted
             playsinline
             disablepictureinpicture
             disableremoteplayback
             x-webkit-airplay="deny"></video>

      <div class="pvid-lb-bar">
        <button type="button" class="pvid-lb-btn" data-pvid-lb-sound aria-label="Turn sound on">
          <span class="pvid-i pvid-i--soundoff">{% render 'icon', name: 'sound-off', size: 20 %}</span>
          <span class="pvid-i pvid-i--soundon">{% render 'icon', name: 'sound-on', size: 20 %}</span>
        </button>
        <button type="button" class="pvid-lb-btn" data-pvid-lb-close aria-label="Close video viewer">
          {% render 'icon', name: 'close', size: 20 %}
        </button>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
{%- endif -%}

{% schema %}
{
  "name": "Product videos",
  "settings": [
    { "type": "text", "id": "heading", "label": "Heading" },
    { "type": "text", "id": "jump_label", "label": "Jump-nav label" },
    {
      "type": "select",
      "id": "surface",
      "label": "Surface",
      "options": [
        { "value": "chalk", "label": "Chalk" },
        { "value": "jute", "label": "Jute" }
      ],
      "default": "chalk"
    }
  ],
  "presets": [{ "name": "Product videos" }]
}
{% endschema %}
